  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Africa - Technical Capacity for Nutrition</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The proposed IDF grant aims to build technical capacity for the delivery of essential nutrition interventions at the facility and community levels in Tanzania, Uganda and Kenya.  Despite striking improvements in many health indicators over the past decade, there has been limited progress in improving the nutritional status of children and women.  The governments of the three countries have committed to addressing the historic neglect of nutrition and signed up to the global Scaling Up Nutrition  movement.  An assessment conducted in the three countries underscored the need to strengthen the capacity of frontline workers to address nutrition in the health and agriculture sectors.  Discussions of the report resulted in an agreement to pull forces to design and develop training programs that could be adapted and scaled up in each country. To this end, the IDF grant aims to: (i) develop and pilot in-service training programs for health facility and community based workers; (ii) design and develop a pre-service training program for entry level health personnel; and (iii) share lessons and experiences among the three countries and mobilize high-level support from key stakeholders to expand and scale up the training programs.  The activities would be coordinated by the East, Central, and Southern Africa Health Community, which is overseeing similar Bank and non-Bank activities.  The proposed activities are in line with Bank-supported operations in these countries and would contribute to the attainment of the country strategy objectives to strengthen human capital and to promote equitable growth.</narrative>
  </description>
